INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - NIGHT

1862. A lamp burns low. Wires hum along the ceiling. A key clatters on the desk.

A YOUNG OPERATOR, 20s, in sleeve garters and a green eyeshade, hunches over the key. He is the only one in the room. He taps back an answer, grinning at whatever comes in.

A SOUNDER chatters. He copies fast. Reads aloud.

                    OPERATOR
"Mother recovering. Come home Sunday." Well, that's a relief.

He taps a reply. Paper curls beneath his fingers. Then the sounder changes tempo. Slow. Cold. He freezes and copies each letter as it comes.

                    OPERATOR (CONT'D)
"Enemy cavalry crossing at Harper's Ford. Hold for orders."

He pulls the paper loose. Stands. Sits again. He reads it over, mouthing the words.

The line crackles. A new voice, ghostly, through the speaker of an experimental telephone beside the key.

                    COLONEL (V.O.)
(over line)
Operator! Report!

He lunges for the receiver, nearly knocking the lamp.

                    OPERATOR
Sir! Message from Harper's Ford! They've crossed!

                    COLONEL (V.O.)
(over line)
How many?

                    OPERATOR
It doesn't say, sir!

                    COLONEL (V.O.)
(over line)
Then find out, son!

Shaking, he taps. Waits. The sounder is silent. He taps again. Silent.

The lamp gutters.

He sets down the receiver, very gently, and sits with his hands flat on the desk. After a long moment he speaks without turning.

                    OPERATOR
Whoever you are on the other end... I hope you ran.

The wire hums on, empty.
